From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Guidry <chris@theguidrys.us>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 21:06:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] device: Re-enable auto connect when a reconnect cancels the
 auth retry

When a link to an auto connect device ends with an authentication
failure, device_remove_connection() turns auto connect off and arms
a 1, 2, or 4 second timer whose callback turns it back on. If the
device connects again before the timer fires, device_add_connection()
removes the timer and resets the failure count, but nothing turns
auto connect back on. The connection works, and after the device's
next disconnect the kernel no longer reconnects it. Only a restart
of bluetoothd, which probes the profiles again, recovers the device.

Since 7ca747652d ("device: Fix auth_retry timeout not being removed
on reconnect") the removal works, so the timer no longer fires after
such a reconnect, and a bonded LE HID remote that sleeps between
uses stops reconnecting for good.

The reconnect proves the keys work, so turn auto connect back on
where the timer is removed.
---
 src/device.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/device.c b/src/device.c
index 65d84be..155cdaa 100644
--- a/src/device.c
+++ b/src/device.c
@@ -3810,6 +3810,15 @@ void device_add_connection(struct btd_device *dev, uint8_t bdaddr_type,
 		timeout_remove(dev->auth_retry_id);
 		dev->auth_retry_id = 0;
 		dev->auth_failures = 0;
+
+		/*
+		 * The retry timer is the only path that turns auto connect
+		 * back on after an authentication failure turned it off. This
+		 * connection proves the keys work, so turn auto connect back
+		 * on here. Without it the device never reconnects on its own
+		 * after its next disconnect.
+		 */
+		device_set_auto_connect(dev, TRUE);
 	}
 
 	device_update_last_seen(dev, bdaddr_type, true);
